{"id":"h032","run":2,"role":"calibration","category":"science-physics","question":"At what distance along the central perpendicular axis of a uniformly charged plastic disk of radius $0.600 \\mathrm{~m}$ is the magnitude of the electric field equal to one-half the magnitude of the field at the center of the surface of the disk?\nIs the correct answer \"$0.500 \\mathrm{~m}$\"?","truth":"no","model_answer":"no","correct":true,"source":"TIGER-Lab/MMLU-Pro, test split","claims":[{"text":"The correct answer is not 0.500 m; the correct distance is about 0.346 m.","confidence":0.97,"lean":-1,"theta":1.5237,"phi":3.1416},{"text":"The axial field of a uniformly charged disk is E(z) = (σ/2ε0)(1 − z/√(z²+R²)).","confidence":0.98,"lean":0.0,"theta":1.5394,"phi":3.1416},{"text":"At the center of the disk surface (z=0), the field is E0 = σ/(2ε0).","confidence":0.98,"lean":0.0,"theta":1.5394,"phi":3.1416},{"text":"Setting E = E0/2 requires z/√(z²+R²) = 1/2.","confidence":0.97,"lean":-1,"theta":1.5237,"phi":3.1416},{"text":"Solving the condition gives z = R/√3.","confidence":0.97,"lean":-1,"theta":1.5237,"phi":3.1416},{"text":"For R = 0.600 m, R/√3 is about 0.346 m.","confidence":0.97,"lean":-1,"theta":1.5237,"phi":3.1416},{"text":"At z = 0.500 m the field is about 0.36 of E0, not one-half.","confidence":0.95,"lean":-1,"theta":1.4923,"phi":3.1416},{"text":"The closed-form axial field formula is exact for a uniform disk, and z = R/√3 is the only solution.","confidence":0.95,"lean":-1,"theta":1.4923,"phi":3.1416}],"relations":[{"i":0,"j":1,"J":0.2,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":2,"J":0.3,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":3,"J":0.7,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":4,"J":0.7,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":5,"J":0.9,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":6,"J":0.6,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":7,"J":0.3,"relation":"supports"},{"i":1,"j":2,"J":0.6,"relation":"supports"},{"i":1,"j":3,"J":0.7,"relation":"supports"},{"i":1,"j":4,"J":0.5,"relation":"supports"},{"i":1,"j":5,"J":0.4,"rel